problems.problem:
$ABC$ is a triangle with side lengths $BC = 12$, $CA = 11$, $AB = 5$. For a real number $k$ with $0 < k < 1$, let
• $P_1$ be the point dividing the side $BC$ internally in the ratio $k : 1 - k$, and $P_2$ the point dividing it internally in the ratio $1 - k : k$;
• $Q_1$ be the point dividing the side $CA$ internally in the ratio $6 : 1$, and $Q_2$ the point dividing it internally in the ratio $1 : 6$;
• $R_1$ be the point dividing the side $AB$ internally in the ratio $2 : 5$, and $R_2$ the point dividing it internally in the ratio $5 : 2$.
When a triangle $PQR$ with side lengths $QR = Q_1R_2$, $RP = R_1P_2$, $PQ = P_1Q_2$ exists, find the smallest possible value of $\cos\angle QPR$.

Positive integers $a, b, c$ satisfy the following 4 conditions:
• the greatest common divisor of $a, b, c$ is 1;
• the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $b + c$ is greater than 1;
• the greatest common divisor of $b$ and $c + a$ is greater than 1;
• the greatest common divisor of $c$ and $a + b$ is greater than 1.
Find the smallest possible value of $a + b + c$.
